---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import MovingOsContextCta from '../../components/MovingOsContextCta.astro';
import FurnitureFitTool from '../../components/FurnitureFitTool.astro';
import '../../styles/uiux-prototypes.css';
import { en } from '../../i18n/en';

const title = 'Furniture Fit Checker | Room Fit & Clearance';
const description = 'Check whether a sofa, bed, desk, wardrobe or table fits inside a room and leaves the clearance you need. Check doors, halls, stairs and elevators separately.';
const canonical = '/en/furniture-fit-checker/';
const alternates = { zh: '/zh/furniture-fit-checker/', en: '/en/furniture-fit-checker/' };
const siteUrl = 'https://roomfeng.win';
const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Furniture Fit Checker for Room Fit and Clearance',
    description,
    inLanguage: 'en',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'RoomFeng' },
    mainEntityOfPage: `${siteUrl}${canonical}`,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${siteUrl}/en/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Furniture fit checker', item: `${siteUrl}${canonical}` },
    ],
  },
];
const faq = [
  { q: 'What measurements do I need before using the furniture fit checker?', a: 'Measure the clear room length and width, the furniture width and depth, fixed obstacles, and the everyday space needed for doors, drawers, chairs, and walking.' },
  { q: 'Should I use product dimensions or packaging dimensions?', a: 'Use product or bed-frame dimensions for room placement. Check packaging, doorway, hallway, stair, and elevator measurements separately for delivery-route planning.' },
  { q: 'How much clearance should I leave around furniture?', a: 'About 60 cm can be a practical starting target for a small-room route, but chairs, wardrobe doors, drawers, beds, and entry zones may need more.' },
  { q: 'Does this tool prove a furniture item will fit through a hallway?', a: 'No. This tool checks an in-room footprint and requested clearances; it does not model stair turns, elevator geometry, hallway-corner rotation, or a full delivery route.' },
];
const relatedLinks = [
  { href: '/en/room-layout-planner/', title: 'Room layout planner' },
  { href: '/en/moving-furniture-size-check/', title: 'Moving furniture size check' },
  { href: '/en/apartment-furniture-planner/', title: 'Apartment furniture planner' },
  { href: '/en/small-bedroom-layout-planner/', title: 'Small bedroom layout planner' },
  { href: '/en/furniture-fit-calculator/', title: 'Quick furniture fit calculator' },
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} lang="en" alternates={alternates} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container page-grid">
      <header class="landing-intro card">
        <p class="eyebrow">MEASURE → PLAN → CHECK</p>
        <h1>Furniture fit checker</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Check whether a piece fits inside the destination room and leaves the clearance you want. This tool does not model the full delivery route.
        </p>
        <p>Measure the room and furniture first, then use the checker below to compare the footprint, in-room rotation, and requested everyday clearance.</p>
        <p>Need to check doors, hallways, stairs or an elevator? <a href="/en/moving-furniture-size-check/">Measure the full delivery route</a>.</p>
      </header>

      <FurnitureFitTool locale="en" />

      <article class="card seo-article">
        <p class="lead">
          Before you buy a bed, desk, wardrobe, sofa, or dining table, check the real room size and the everyday clearances around it. A piece can technically fit in a room and still be wrong for the way you live.
        </p>
        <p>
          Furniture fit is not only a width-by-depth question. The item has to sit in the room, leave an everyday route from the door, allow drawers or wardrobe doors to open, and avoid blocking windows, radiators, outlets, or the only comfortable walkway. Buyers often compare product dimensions with one wall and stop there. That catches obvious problems, but it misses what happens when a person needs to walk past the bed, pull out a chair, open storage, or carry laundry through the room.
        </p>
        <p>
          Start by measuring the room at floor level, not from a listing photo. Note the usable wall lengths after subtracting doors, built-in closets, low windows, columns, pipes, or baseboard heaters. Then measure the furniture itself. For a bed, include the full frame, not only the mattress. For a wardrobe, include door swing or drawer pull-out depth. For a desk, include chair space behind it. For a sofa, check both the length along the wall and the depth into the room, because a deep sofa can make a narrow living room feel blocked even when the length looks fine.
        </p>

        <h2>Sizing and clearance tips</h2>
        <p>
          Use about 60 cm as a practical minimum walking route in a small room. More is better near a bed side, wardrobe, desk chair, dining chair, or entry. If a route is below that, the room may still be usable, but carrying a bag, making the bed, vacuuming, or moving past another person can become frustrating. Leave space for door arcs and keep the entry clear before adding optional pieces such as side tables or storage carts.
        </p>
        <p>
          For large purchases, check the delivery path separately rather than treating this checker as a route calculator. Measure the front door, elevator, stairwell, hallway turns, and tight corners. A sofa with removable legs or a bed frame that arrives flat-packed is a different delivery problem from an assembled cabinet. Use the <a href="/en/moving-furniture-size-check/">moving furniture size check</a> for that ordered route review.
        </p>

        <h2>How to do this with the planner</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Open the <a href="/en/room-layout-planner/">room layout planner</a> and enter the room length and width in cm, m, or ft.</li>
          <li>Add the door first, then place windows or fixed obstacles so the usable walls are clear.</li>
          <li>Add the furniture you are checking and edit its width, depth, and rotation to match the product dimensions.</li>
          <li>Drag the item through likely positions and watch for out-of-room, blocked-door, and narrow-walkway warnings.</li>
          <li>Export a PNG or PDF to compare with the product page, store listing, or moving checklist.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Treat the result as a practical in-room planning draft. If the layout only works when furniture touches every wall and the walkway warning stays visible, consider a smaller size, a different orientation, or a furniture piece with built-in storage that replaces another item. A separate route check is still required before delivery.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="cta-band">
        <div>
          <h2>Check the furniture size in a real floor plan</h2>
          <p>Use your room measurements and the product dimensions to test fit before ordering or arranging delivery.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="button" href="/en/room-layout-planner/">{en.cta.openPlanner}</a>
      </div>
      <MovingOsContextCta locale="en" placement="en_furniture_fit" heading="Checking one piece or planning the whole move?" copy="A fit check answers one item. Moving &amp; New Home OS helps you decide what comes with you, where it lands, how it gets packed, and what the move will cost." label="Plan the whole move" />
      <AdSlot id="en-fit-mid" placement="en-furniture-fit-mid" />
      <Faq lang="en" items={faq} />
      <RelatedLinks lang="en" links={relatedLinks} />
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
